`this.commandLine.fileNames.includes(fileName)` is O(n) inside a `for` loop, making `ensureFiles` O(n²) in the number of already-tracked files. For large projects (Mantine has 133 components across many story files) this will be called frequently with growing lists. A `Set<string>` shadow alongside the array would make membership checks O(1):
```ts
const fileSet = new Set(this.commandLine.fileNames);
for (const fileName of fileNames) {
  if (!fileSet.has(fileName)) {
    this.commandLine.fileNames.push(fileName);
    fileSet.add(fileName);
    added = true;
  }
}
```

The `catch` block is completely silent. Any exception during type resolution (null-deref inside a TS API, unexpected symbol shape, etc.) produces empty props with no trace. Even a single `logger.debug` line would make debugging failed extractions much easier:
```ts
} catch (err) {
  logger.debug(`[reactComponentMeta] Failed to extract props for ${entry.componentPath}: ${err}`);
  continue;
}
```
